-- title:
--   Normality of the descended supersingular special fibre, q=3
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ denotes `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and for a valuation subring $A$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ and an intermediate field $k_0$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ the ring $A_0 :=$ `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))` is the induced valuation subring of $k_0$; $\pi_0 \in k_0$ with $\pi_0 \in A$ provides the distinguished element $\langle \pi_0\rangle$ of $A_0$.
--
--   **Arithmetic frame.** A prime $q$ with $q = 3$; a nonzero level $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$; a guard prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$; a valuation subring $A \subset \bar{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a nonunit of $A$ (`LiesOverPrime`). A finite set $W$ of places of the characteristic-$q$ modular function field `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` which, by `hW`, consists exactly of the supersingular places: those places $w$ that are rational, are affine geometric places, and whose value $w.\mathrm{evalAt}$ at the generator `jGeomGen` lies in `ssJSet q (ResidueField A)`, the set of $j$ for which every elliptic curve with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point. A chosen $s \in W$. A constant reduction datum $R_0$ (a valuation subring of the base-changed modular function field `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, a surjective residue map onto `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` with kernel the maximal ideal, compatibility with $A$, and a degree-preserving map on places), together with `hR₀`, which says that a Laurent series over $A$ whose image in $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` lies in $R_0$.`integers` and has $R_0$-residue equal to its coefficientwise reduction modulo the maximal ideal of $A$; and `hle`, asserting `modularFunctionFieldBar M' ≤ fieldBar q M'`.
--
--   **Local hypotheses on the coefficient ring.** $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, is Henselian, has algebraically closed residue field, and (`hκ`) every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0 \cap A$.
--
--   **Auxiliary constants.** A prime $\ell \ge 3$ with $\ell \ne q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$, and $\zeta_0 \in k_0$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity; $\varpi_t \in k_0$ lying in $A$ with $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\cdot u$ for a unit $u$ of $A$, and $\varpi_t' \in A_0$ with $\varpi_t' = \varpi_t$; an intermediate field $K_b$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ with $K_b = \bot$, a valuation subring $A_b$ of $K_b$ whose elements are exactly those lying in $A$, and a nonzero $\varpi_b$ generating the maximal ideal of $A_b$; tameness, in the form that $\ell_g - 1$ is a unit of $A_0$; and $\xi \in k_0$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity.
--
--   **Function field and chart.** $H_1 =$ `levelH q M'` $\cap\ \ker\big((\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times\big)$; $K_\ell$ an intermediate field of `LaurentSeries ↥k₀` over $k_0$, carrying an $A_0$-algebra structure compatible with the tower, equal to the base change `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`; a nonzero $j_\ell \in K_\ell$ equal to the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion `jq`; and `hjK`, that `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` lies in $K_\ell$. The chart is `chartAlgFin A₀ K_ℓ jℓ`, the $A_0$-subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $A_0[j_\ell]$. Data on it: a maximal ideal $y$ containing the image of $\pi_0$; `hss`, that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism from the chart to $\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the image of `jChartFin` lies in `ssJSet q Ω`; `hover`, which identifies $y$ with the place $s$ (for every $g$ in the full modular function field of level $M'$ whose base change lies in $R_0$.`integers`, which has non-negative order at every place of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` where `jq` does, and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every $c \in k_0 \cap A$ whose residue equals the value of that residue at $s$, the difference of $g$ and $c$ lies in $y$); and `hOrbit`, which says that every maximal ideal $y'$ of the chart containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same condition as in `hover` is carried to $y$ by a level automorphism: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau \in \mathrm{Aut}_{k_0}(K_\ell)$ with `IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ Kℓ τ` (the condition that $\tau$ acts on ratios of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ as $\gamma^{-1}$ acts analytically, read through any embedding of $k_0$ into $\mathbb C$ sending $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$) such that $\tau$ preserves the chart and $y'$ is the $\tau$-transport of $y$.
--
--   **The rigid chart hypothesis `hS1`** (a conjunction of thirteen clauses, summarised here) provides a subalgebra $B_t \subseteq K_\ell$ over $A_0$ and a valuation subring $W_t$ of $K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ (the hypothesis `hBW`) such that: the chart is contained in $B_t$ and $K_\ell$ is the field of fractions of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$ and $B_t/\pi_0B_t$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t$ meets $k_0$ exactly in $A_0$, is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, is the localisation of $B_t$ at that maximal ideal, and cuts out $y$ on the chart; for every $\mathrm{GF}(q^2)$-algebra structure on the residue field $k$ of $A_0$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho : B_t \to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) (the quotient of $k[X_0,X_1]$ by `drinfeldPoly q k - 1`) whose kernel is the set of elements of the maximal ideal of $W_t$, which is compatible with $A_0 \to k$, and which is equivariant for level automorphisms: each $\tau$ attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ acts through `hAction` by an element $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ of `hSubgroup q`, with $c \ne 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$, and with the pair $(1,c)$ realised by a further level automorphism attached to some $\gamma' \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; $B_t$ is stable under all level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$; every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ is obtained from the maximal ideal of $W_t$ by a level automorphism attached to $\Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; and an element of $B_t$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W_t$ after every such level automorphism is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   **Inertia and end data.** Natural numbers $n \ge 1$ with $n \mid q+1$ and $n = 2\cdot$`placeWidthChar q M' s` (so $n$ is twice the quotient of `jWidthChar q` at the value of $s$ at `jGeomGen` by the ramification index `placeRamificationJ`); $m \ge 1$ with $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t'\cdot w$ for a unit $w$ of $A_0$; $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau_0$ a level automorphism attached to $\gamma_0^{-1}$ which preserves $W_t$; an element $\zeta_c$ of the adic completion of $A_0$ with $\zeta_c^n = 1$ and $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$; $a_0 \in A_0$ together with `hjC` and `ha₀y`, asserting that `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` lies in the chart and that its difference from $a_0$ lies in $y$; and a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hR7` (a conjunction of twelve clauses, summarised here) requires: $\tau_0^n$ is the identity on $B_t$ while $\tau_0^k$ is not for $0<k<n$, and every level automorphism attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with some $\tau_0^k$, $k<n$; `ends` has exactly $q+1$ elements; level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ permute `ends`, the induced action is transitive, $\tau_0$ preserves each end, and distinct ends are separated by an element which is a nonunit in one and a unit in the other; there is a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_c$ of $K_\ell$, stable under level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, contained in every end, each end being the corresponding localisation of $B_c$; an end whose image under a level automorphism lies in $W_t$ forces that automorphism to preserve $W_t$; and, for each end $O$: $O \subseteq W_t$, $O$ is a Noetherian local ring containing the chart and meeting $k_0$ exactly in $A_0$, every element of $O$ is congruent to a constant in the sense of having nonunit difference from the image of some element of $A_0$, and $O$ admits a crossing-model description — elements $c_x, c_y$, a unit $u$, an isomorphism $\iota$ from the adic completion of $O$ onto `UVCrossingModel` over the adic completion of $A_0$ with parameter $\pi_0^m$, units $\gamma_U,\gamma_V$, an automorphism $\theta_0$, an inverse $\zeta_c'$ of $\zeta_c$, with $c_xc_y = \pi_0^m u$, $\iota$ matching constants, $\iota(c_x) = \gamma_U\cdot U$, $\iota(c_y) = \gamma_V\cdot V$, $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, $\iota$ sending `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` $- a_0$ to a unit times $V^e$ with $e \ge 1$, $\iota$ conjugating $\tau_0$ into $\theta_0$, and $\theta_0(U) \equiv \zeta_c U$, $\theta_0(V) \equiv \zeta_c' V$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal — together with a valuation subring $W_x$ of $K_\ell$ dominating $O$ (a discrete valuation ring with uniformiser $\pi_0$, meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, containing the chart, with Eisenstein-type clauses for a parameter $t$ and for `jqNModC ↥k₀ q`, with $c_x$ in and $c_y$ outside its maximal ideal, detecting $y$ on the chart from both sides, not containing the other ends, and characterised among ends by this property) and a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_x$ of $B_t \cap O$ stable under level automorphisms, with non-negative order wherever $j_\ell$ has, presenting $O$ as a localisation of $B_x$, and containing an element detecting valuation subrings in which $j_\ell$ has transcendental reduction; finally, every valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, containing the chart and with $y$ contained in the contraction of its maximal ideal, either contains $B_t$ or dominates one of the ends.
--
--   The hypothesis `hR9` asserts the existence of $b \in B_t$ lying outside every valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ which meets $k_0$ in $A_0$, has $\pi_0$ in its maximal ideal, and contains $j_\ell$ with no monic $A_0$-polynomial value at $j_\ell$ in its maximal ideal. The hypothesis `hR10` asserts that for every maximal ideal $y''$ of the chart containing $\pi_0$ which is not the transport of $y$ under any level automorphism attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ there is $b \in B_t$ lying outside every valuation subring containing the chart and dominating the corresponding translate of $y''$.
--
--   **Descent data.** $G$ is the subgroup of $\mathrm{Aut}_{k_0}(K_\ell)$ generated by the level automorphisms attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; $K_0$ is the fixed field of $G$; $B_0$ is an $A_0$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$ characterised by $B_0 = \{f \in B_t : \tau f = f \text{ for all } \tau \in G\}$; and $W_0'$ is the valuation subring of $K_0$ consisting of the elements of $K_0$ lying in $W_t$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** The special fibre of the descended chart is integrally closed: the quotient $B_0 / \pi_0 B_0$, by the ideal generated by the image of $\langle\pi_0\rangle \in A_0$, satisfies `IsIntegrallyClosed`.
--
--   This is the normality step for the quotient chart in the analysis of the stable reduction at $q=3$ of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ at a supersingular point: the invariants $B_0 = B_t^{G}$ of the rigid supersingular chart under the level group generated by $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ have integrally closed special fibre. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.AuxLevel.formallySmooth_invariants_of_rigidChart_linkedScalars_of_eq_three_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.AuxLevel.formallySmooth_invariants_of_rigidChart_linkedScalars_of_eq_three_of_dvd), and the proof cites the Galois-descent criterion [`IsGaloisGroup.isIntegrallyClosed_of_isIntegrallyClosed`](thm.html#IsGaloisGroup.isIntegrallyClosed_of_isIntegrallyClosed), the Dedekind property of the Drinfeld coordinate ring, and the uniqueness, existence and finiteness statements for level automorphisms at level $H_1$.
